    category: "Coding Output Challenges",
    expectedAnswer: "For O(n), store each previous value and ask whether target - current already exists.",
    deepExplanation: "Step 1 — Understand the topic.\n\nFor O(n), store each previous value and ask whether target - current already exists.\n\nStep 2 — Easy method:\nIdentify the rule, trace the smallest example, then check edge cases.\n\nStep 3 — WITHOUT BUILT-IN / CORE TypeScript:\n```ts\nfunction twoSum(values: number[], target: number): [number, number] | undefined {\n  const seen: Record<string, number> = {};\n\n  for (let i = 0; i < values.length; i += 1) {\n    const needed = target - values[i];\n\n    if (seen[String(needed)] !== undefined) {\n      return [seen[String(needed)], i];\n    }\n\n    seen[String(values[i])] = i;\n  }\n\n  return undefined;\n}\n\nconsole.log(twoSum([2, 7, 11, 15], 9));\n```\n\nStep 4 — WITH BUILT-IN / PRACTICAL TypeScript:\n```ts\nconst values = [2, 7, 11, 15];\nconst result = values.flatMap((value, i) =>\n  values.slice(i + 1).map((other, j) =>\n    value + other === 9 ? [i, i + 1 + j] : [],\n  ),\n).find(pair => pair.length > 0);\n```\n\nStep 5 — Example result:\n```text\n[0, 1]\n```\n\nStep 6 — Edge cases:\nCheck null/undefined, empty inputs, mutation vs copying, errors, async ordering, browser/Node differences, cancellation, and memory cleanup where relevant.\n\nStep 7 — Senior interview takeaway:\nExplain the semantic rule first, then implementation, complexity, failure mode, and production trade-off.",
